<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lake Trasimene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/217-bce">217 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a> was fought in 217 BCE in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> ambushed a Roman army under the consul Gaius Flaminius along the lake shore, destroying it and killing Flaminius. It was one of the greatest ambushes in military history and a major victory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-trasimene.jpg" alt="Battle of Lake Trasimene" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph-Noël Sylvestre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sylvestre_Ducar_decapite_Flaminius_(Trasimene).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had fought the First Punic War from 264 to 241 BCE. Afterwards the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a> expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthaginian-iberia">Carthaginian Iberia</a> into an autonomous territory ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a>, which gave Carthage silver mines, manpower and shipyards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> succeeded to its rule in 221 BCE. In 219 BCE his army besieged and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Saguntum</a>, which had a treaty with Rome, and Rome declared war.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">New Carthage</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in May 218 BCE and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in October with 20,000 infantry, 6,000 cavalry and elephants. He beat the consul Publius Scipio at the Battle of Ticinus and then destroyed most of a Roman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trebia">Battle of the Trebia</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the consuls Gnaeus Geminus and Gaius Flaminius were elected, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> fielded twenty-two legions in 217 BCE, ten more than the year before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In spring 217 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> by a difficult but unguarded route and moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, plundering, razing villages and killing every adult man they met. Flaminius, commanding the Roman army at Arretium, set off in pursuit. The historian Adrian Goldsworthy notes that his troops, marching through devastated land, would have felt humiliated and provoked. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> thought the Carthaginians were fleeing and pursued so fast that they could not scout properly. On 20 June the Carthaginians marched along the north shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno">Lake Trasimene</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> decided to turn and fight there.</p><p>On 21 June 217 BCE, probably in poor visibility, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were attacked from the flank and rear and had no chance to form even a rudimentary line. After three hours of hard fighting some 15,000 were killed. About 6,000 at the front of the column escaped the trap but were surrounded later that day and surrendered. Nearly all of Flaminius's army of about 25,000 was killed or captured. The destruction of a whole army by an ambush is widely considered unique. Several days later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> wiped out the cavalry of the second Roman army, which did not yet know of the disaster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>News of the defeat caused panic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and Quintus Fabius Maximus Verrucosus was elected dictator. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> marched towards southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, hoping to win over Greek and Italic city-states. Impatient with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fabian-strategy">Fabian strategy</a> of avoiding major battles, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> elected Lucius Paullus and Gaius Varro as consuls the next year. They fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Battle of Cannae</a> in 216 BCE, a third and worse disaster, and thirteen more years of war followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lake Trasimene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Three crushing Roman defeats followed: the Trebia in 218 BCE, <b>Lake Trasimene</b> in 217 BCE and above all Cannae in 216 BCE, where Hannibal’s double envelopment of a Roman army of perhaps 80,000 men killed an estimated 47,000–70,000 in a single afternoon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 217 BCE, after Hannibal's victory at the <b>Battle of Lake Trasimene</b>, it was reformed with sacrifices in the Greek manner.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
